WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:09.510
In the world of "Joker", today's theory will show that the biggest joke of all... is on us.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:22.580
Hello Internet! Welcome to Film Theory, where we just encourage you to smile.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:27.200
Just keep on smiling, keep smiling, keep SMILING.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:27.960
Now I gotta be honest,

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:32.940
when I first saw an announcement about a Joker solo movie shortly after the release of Suicide Squad,

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.709
I thought it was all one bad joke.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:41.040
I mean, let's just say that most people weren't super thrilled with that movie's characterization of such an iconic villain.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.440
"this handsome HUNKA-HUNKA!"

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.440
"You don't want no beef? You don't want no beef?"

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.860
"I SMELL LIKE BEEEEF"

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:54.740
So coming back for yet another version of the character so soon was... a.. surprise to say the least,

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:57.420
then the trailer came out and I was stunned.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:04.240
This wasn't gonna be a film about billionaire superheroes with bat nipples beating up thugs in rainbow colored streets,

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:07.660
instead it was gonna be a dive into the psychology of a man.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.020
A man named Arthur Fleck.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:11.160
A man plagued by mental illness,

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.440
a man forgotten by government and abused by society,

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.920
a man just like any other, completely unremarkable

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:19.800
...except for one small detail.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:27.200
We're told by the title of the movie that he's gonna rise to take the title of comics' greatest super villain, the Joker.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.700
It's the sort of deconstruction of the genre that I love,

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.140
and that I think we're all desperate for.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:35.250
Don't get me wrong, I love the spectacle of superhero movies,

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:39.780
but after more than a decade of flashy CGI battles with big villains

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.060
(and even bigger budgets),

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:45.100
what gets me most excited are the ones brave enough to show us something darker,

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.020
stuff like "Logan" or "The Boys",

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:49.180
stories set in universes of superheroes,

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:52.120
but where good and evil aren't so clear-cut.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.600
Where the heroes aren't always the good guys.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:02:02.340
Films that don't just gloss over the rotten underbelly of the society that needs heroes to rescue it in the first place,

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.499
and Joker seems to tick all those boxes.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.800
But there was something else that I found interesting about Joker when watching the trailers.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.440
I don't think it's real.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.220
Now obviously, the movie exists,

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:13.540
that's not what I'm saying here.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:19.180
No, what I mean to say is that anytime you have a film telling such a close personal story of a character,

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.340
who's clearly struggling with mental illness,

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.934
it leaves open the  possibility for misdirection,

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.900
for the film to suddenly descend into hallucinations.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:32.440
For the camera itself to be an unreliable narrator as it depicts not reality as it's truly happening,

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.840
but rather reality as its characters,

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:37.620
or in this case, Arthur Fleck, wants to see it.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:38.659
For an example of what I mean,

00:02:38.659 --> 00:02:39.940
just look at Fight Club,

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:44.520
the ultimate example of a movie that's lying to you the entire time.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:45.900
And the more I looked into the trailers,

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.380
interviews around this film,

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:48.880
and the source material that inspired it,

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:50.480
the more convinced I became.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:54.400
What we're going to end up seeing in "Joker" isn't going to be entirely real.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.360
The movie, or at least a sizable portion of it,

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.200
is gonna be playing out in Joker's own mind.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:02.209
What we're gonna end up seeing on screen

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.900
may not be what's actually happening in Gotham around the setting of the movie.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:09.380
But how, and why?

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:10.960
Let's look a bit closer, shall we?

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:13.260
Let's start by dissecting Arthur himself.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.600
The trailers introduce us to Arthur Fleck,

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.888
which, it's worth noting, is a new name for Joker's character.

00:03:18.889 --> 00:03:23.360
No official name has ever been given to the Joker in the main canon DC Comics,

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.960
and Jack Napier from the Tim Burton movies,

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.688
and the alternate universe "White Knight" is about as close as we've ever come before.

00:03:29.688 --> 00:03:34.840
In fact, the team working on "Joker" made it pretty explicit that this story is entirely original,

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:38.700
so no digging through deep comic lore is gonna be helping us out this time around.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:43.420
It's immediately communicated to us from the trailers that Arthur is suffering from some form of mental illness.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:49.260
Not only do we see him in therapy, but if you look close at his notepad it outright confirms it.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:49.760
Reading

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:55.480
"The worst part about having a mental illness is people expect you to behave as if you don't."

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:58.980
This is also confirmed by numerous interviews done with the casting crew of the film.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:02.560
According to actor Marc Maron in an interview with website NME,

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:06.180
"The approach that Todd Phillips has taken is more of an origin story,"

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:10.860
"and a character study of a mentally ill person that becomes the Joker."

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:16.420
So we know that Arthur Fleck is dealing with some mental disorder, but what sort of illness is it?

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:19.240
Clearly not all mental illnesses function the same way,

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:22.920
so to better understand this character, and thus the movie he's a part of,

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:25.640
we have to first understand his diagnosis.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:30.460
Looking at the trailers, we see a few curious details that could be symptoms of Arthur's illness.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:34.140
First, he appears to have inappropriate emotional responses.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:37.460
Laughing without any sort of stimulus, laughing after being beat up,

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:40.030
switching into and out of laughter instantly.

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:41.930
*laughing*

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:47.683
And while out of nowhere uncontrollable laughter is indeed a real medical condition,

00:04:47.683 --> 00:04:49.600
known as "Pseudobulbar Affect",

00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:53.500
there are more symptoms here that don't line up with that initial diagnosis.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:57.640
Symptoms such as moments of violent outbursts or inappropriate actions,

00:04:57.640 --> 00:04:59.800
smashing himself against protective screens,

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:01.480
smashing himself against mirrors,

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:02.529
painting his own tongue.

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:05.640
Generally speaking, Arthur's movements feel awkward.

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:08.840
Watching him walk and dance in both trailers feels uncomfortable.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.000
Feels disjointed.

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:10.740
He's jerky.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:12.609
His feet are moving in strange patterns.

00:05:12.609 --> 00:05:15.500
The illness also seems to be hereditary,

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:18.760
with his mother needing at-home care, despite appearing rather young,

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:20.720
and in what otherwise would seem to be good health.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:21.760
All of these symptoms

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:27.380
Seem to point to Joker's vague mental illness being more precisely diagnosed as Schizophrenia,

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:32.740
a chronic mental disorder characterized by a breakdown between what someone perceives as reality

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:34.900
and what's really happening around them.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:40.180
Look at this list of characteristics and think back to what we just talked about with regards to Arthur.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:41.680
Inexact motor skills,

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:43.240
jerking arm movements,

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:44.300
awkward gait,

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:46.260
unusual gestures or postures,

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:47.760
feelings of detachment,

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:48.640
sudden anger,

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:49.560
social isolation,

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:55.540
it's almost an exact match for the behavior that we see Arthur exhibiting throughout these two trailers.

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:59.820
And Schizophrenia does indeed have a strong genetic component.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:04.400
Even the handwriting on his joke pad is a clue to his real diagnosis here.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:08.040
Notice how the writing gets more and more disorganized as it goes on?

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:11.120
Well, compare that to what we see in this study from the paper,

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:14.120
"Differences in Handwritings of Schizophrenia Patients"

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:17.680
and you can clearly see the exact same style of traits,

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:20.019
the size and pressure of the pen marks differ,

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:22.510
it's messy and outside the bounds of the lines,

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:24.220
the letters are poorly formed.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:27.560
Without treatment, people with schizophrenia tend to have problems in society

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:30.180
because of a feeling that they've lost touch with reality,

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:34.900
in large part due to Schizophrenia's key symptom, delusions and hallucinations.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:39.160
And it's those two symptoms that seem really pertinent to our theory today,

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:42.520
about what is and isn't real in this new Joker movie.

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:44.860
You see in the lead up to the premiere of Joker,

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:48.020
you keep seeing one other movie constantly being mentioned.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:52.580
1982's "The King of Comedy", a film directed by Martin Scorsese.

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:55.100
Now, for as famous of a director as Martin Scorsese is,

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:59.880
delivering iconic movies like "Goodfellas", "Taxi Driver", "The Departed" and "Raging Bull",

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:03.120
"King of Comedy" tends not to be one of his better known works.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:08.440
And yet here it is, being cited as a key point of inspiration for what we'll end up seeing in "Joker".

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:13.400
"There's a connection obviously with the whole thing, but it's not as a direct connection."

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:16.860
This is according to actor Robert De Niro, who plays the main character,

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:19.020
a struggling stand-up comic in "King of Comedy",

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:19.900
and, wouldn't you know it,

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:24.310
is also a major stand-up comic TV show host that we see in "Joker".

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:27.820
But outside of sharing this one actor in somewhat similar roles

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:31.300
in a setting of a New York style city in a crime filled 1980's,

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:33.980
the connections between the two films seem to go deeper.

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:35.060
Much deeper.

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:37.800
In "King of Comedy" we have ourselves Rupert Pupkin,

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:40.509
a socially awkward and mentally ill comedian.

00:07:40.509 --> 00:07:44.140
*laughing*

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:46.820
"Always coming up with these great lines, I love 'em!"

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:49.100
Who idolizes a stand-up TV host,

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:53.840
eventually turning violent as he kidnaps and holds the TV host hostage,

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:56.460
just so he can get his 15 minutes of fame on the show.

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:57.380
"I have a gun..."

00:07:59.420 --> 00:08:00.560
"...at my head..."

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:03.500
"...if a man who identifies himself as...

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:05.600
...the King...

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:08.900
"Hold on, I'm reading from cue cards."

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:11.200
"Tonight's show..."

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:15.220
"...you'll never see me...

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:18.160
"...alive...again."

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:21.960
"It...it''s not grammatically correct, but I think you have the idea."

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:27.260
He does this, at least in part, to impress a young woman named Rita that he finds himself in love with.

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:29.300
And what do we see in the "Joker" trailers?

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:32.880
Well, we have ourselves a socially awkward, mentally ill comedian,

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:35.980
who becomes a villain that we know is prone to violence,

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:41.220
a villain who somehow manages to wind up on the TV talk show of a famous TV comic,

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:44.660
a man who, based on the trailers, is trying to use his comedy

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:48.180
to impress a young woman that he happens to have romantic feelings for.

00:08:48.319 --> 00:08:52.179
So we have ourselves a lot of key plot elements shared between the two movies,

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:55.780
but even some scenes feel like they're direct parallels.

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:57.460
Both characters live with their mothers,

00:08:57.460 --> 00:09:01.100
"Rupert! Are you crazy, what's the matter with you?"

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:02.620
"C'mon mom!"

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:04.220
"People are sleeping, lower it!"

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:07.460
Both characters have exact wishes for how they'd like to be introduced.

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:12.200
"Now, this is my introduction and I'd like Mr. Randall if he could speak it word for word, I would appreciate that."

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:14.020
"This is Randy's introduction to your monologue?"

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:14.520
"Yes it is."

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:15.700
"One small thing."

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:16.480
"Yeah."

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:20.440
"When you bring me out, can you introduce me as Joker?"

00:09:20.809 --> 00:09:24.860
Even shots like this of the Joker getting ready to walk out on stage,

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:27.959
is similar to a motif of shots used throughout "King of Comedy."

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:31.340
"Right now, Jerry is strapped to a chair, somewhere in the middle of this city."

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:32.440
"Go ahead, laugh!"

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:33.600
"Thank you, I appreciate it!"

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:36.520
"Now tomorrow, you'll know that I wasn't kidding and you'll think I was crazy,"

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:40.680
"But look, I figure it this way: Better to be king for a night then schmuck for a lifetime."

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:42.040
*applause*

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:44.280
So why do all these parallels matter?

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:46.100
Well, throughout "King of Comedy"

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:51.760
mixed in with the real-life events of Rupert kidnapping his TV idol, are Rupert's fantasies.

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:56.169
His delusions are blended seamlessly with the scenes that are happening around them,

00:09:56.170 --> 00:09:59.500
There's no visual cue that we're suddenly in the middle of one of Roberts fantasies,

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:03.060
no special filters, no dream sequence fade, nothing.

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:05.060
"There you go, sweetheart."

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:06.860
"Thanks Mr. Pupkin."

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:07.860
"Don't mention it."

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:11.140
"Rupert! Rupert, who are you talking to?"

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:12.520
"Mom!"

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:13.380
"What is it?

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.800
"Please stop calling me!"

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:17.200
"It's terrific. It's great."

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:22.360
The only way that you can tell that these are Rupert's delusions is because of the subject matter.

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:24.680
Rupert is best friends with the TV host.

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:25.990
He gets married to Rita,

00:10:25.990 --> 00:10:28.140
he charms the audience on the show.

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:33.720
And this technique of mixing the fantasy and the reality throws the entire ending of the movie into question.

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:37.400
We're told that Rupert gets out of jail and becomes a wildly successful

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:40.179
because of all the press that his kidnapping scheme generated,

00:10:40.179 --> 00:10:44.260
But again, this is a movie where hallucination and reality blur.

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:47.300
Is this all in his mind as he sits there in his prison cell?

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:48.878
It could be, but it could also not be.

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:51.140
The ending leaves it ambiguous.

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:55.240
"Rupert Pupkin, ladies and gentlemen, let's hear it for Rupert Pupkin!"

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:58.420
"Wonderful! Rupert Pupkin, ladies and gentlemen!"

00:10:58.420 --> 00:11:01.420
At the same time it raises questions about "Joker",

00:11:01.420 --> 00:11:02.700
and what we've seen in the trailers.

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:08.269
Certainly, the dark scenes of Arthur being beat up seem to jive with the reality of an 80's era Gotham,

00:11:08.269 --> 00:11:12.060
but what about the scenes in which massive crowds all wear clown masks,

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:13.577
while protesting the government?

00:11:13.577 --> 00:11:17.560
At first glance, one might think that this is a pretty typical scene in a Batman film.

00:11:17.569 --> 00:11:20.820
The main villain has enthralled the city with some kind of political message.

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:22.248
Perhaps turning them against the Bat.

00:11:22.249 --> 00:11:24.700
But this isn't a Batman film.

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:27.259
That little kid right there, that is our future Batman.

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:32.160
So it seems highly unlikely that he's gonna suit up to take down Mr. J.

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:35.800
So that leads to the question, what's really happening here?

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:38.860
Has Gotham actually turned against the Waynes in this movie?

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:42.660
Or is Arthur fantasizing about some kind of grand revenge,

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:45.799
where everyone in Gotham takes up his image and fights the power?

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:48.769
We have some hints that it might be that latter option.

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:53.620
Throughout the trailers, we're treated to two very different images of Arthur as a clown.

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:57.000
One makes him look like the sign twirler he appears to be at the beginning of the trailer,

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:00.580
while the other makes him look like the Joker as we traditionally know him.

00:12:00.580 --> 00:12:05.000
Whenever he's in the more standard suit, Arthur's life seems to be grounded in reality.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:07.380
He lives in poverty, he cares for his mother.

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:08.659
He's barely making ends meet.

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:11.040
He's struggling to become a stand-up comic.

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:14.540
But whenever we see him in his Joker suit, suddenly everything changes.

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:15.180
He's on stage,

00:12:15.269 --> 00:12:18.460
he's walking through crowds of people dressed like him.

00:12:18.460 --> 00:12:20.280
He's dancing around on stairs.

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:23.920
Everything he does in this Joker suit seems to be his goals.

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:24.800
His fantasies.

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:27.200
Which is why I believe they're exactly that.

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:29.840
Fantasies, delusions, hallucinations.

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:32.920
If he were truly the leader of this rebellion,

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:35.620
would he be allowed to walk through the crowd in his clown makeup?

00:12:35.629 --> 00:12:38.520
While everyone is protesting in one direction,

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:41.220
would he just be allowed to walk in the other direction?

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:46.360
Consider this: all the signs in the crowd are about capitalism in the working-class.

00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:49.540
Pause the trailers and progress frame by frame through them,

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:53.060
and you can see these protests are for workers rights.

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:58.180
Does that seem to be the type of message that Arthur Fleck, the Joker, would be the figurehead for?

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:02.740
Or is he just inserting himself into these protests as some sort of power fantasy?

00:13:02.740 --> 00:13:05.058
This scene of him kissing the girl that he's trying to impress,

00:13:05.059 --> 00:13:10.059
does that sort of confident behavior look like it's coming from a man as shy and mousy as Arthur?

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:12.580
I don't think so, I think these are all part of his hallucinations.

00:13:12.580 --> 00:13:16.220
The delusional world where he lives because of his mental illness.

00:13:16.220 --> 00:13:18.680
As he himself says in the final trailer:

00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:23.500
"For my whole life, I didn't know if I even really existed."

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:27.840
Let's face it, the comic book industry is no stranger to misleading trailers.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:31.680
Heck Marvel's turned digitally altering their trailers into something of a sport,

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:34.680
giving us a two-eyed Thor in "Ragnarok"s trailer,

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:37.220
2D Hulk-ing of Mark Ruffalo in the "Endgame" trailers,

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:38.580
amongst dozens of other changes.

00:13:38.839 --> 00:13:45.180
Remember, I even made a theory about where the Soul Stone was based entirely on digitally edited shots from "Infinity War".

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:46.510
Yeah, thanks Marvel!

00:13:46.510 --> 00:13:48.339
Not gonna forget about that one anytime soon!

00:13:48.339 --> 00:13:52.719
But showing straight-up fantasies from the movie is a brilliant way to misdirect fans.

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:55.080
DC has even used this tactic before,

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:58.380
showing an intimidating Superman in "Batman vs. Superman"s trailer,

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:01.260
that actually just came from one of Batman's dream sequences.

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:04.809
Because we know that the Joker is gonna struggle with mental illness in this movie,

00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:09.640
There's some good reason to believe that he's not gonna be entirely connected to reality.

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:15.380
I think there's a very strong possibility that every time we see Arthur as the Joker in this new movie,

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:16.840
it'll only be in his head.

00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:17.560
A delusion.

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:23.589
We know that there's a villain called Joker at some point, and that there is a clown themed rebellion in Gotham,

00:14:23.589 --> 00:14:25.120
but it's about labor disputes.

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:29.280
The trailers want us to believe that Arthur is the mastermind behind it all,

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:31.060
which I'm not so sure about.

00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:36.140
Arthur Fleck just doesn't seem to be a social engineer like the Joker the trailers are making him out to be.

00:14:36.380 --> 00:14:40.880
Arthur is a tragic dreamer just like Rupert in "King of Comedy" before him.

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:45.460
Men whose greatest achievements exists sadly inside their own minds.

00:14:45.460 --> 00:14:47.170
Or who knows, I could be wrong.

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:51.440
But based on some early reviews that came out literally as we were editing this video,

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:56.880
"One of "Joker" strengths is that anyone will be able to argue their side about what was real and what was imagined."

00:14:56.880 --> 00:15:00.120
It certainly seems like our theory may have predicted the punchline.

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:02.120
*laughing*

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:06.840
But hey, that's just a theory- a Film Theory!

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:09.220
Aaaaand cut.

